---
/**
 * Light/dark toggle.
 *
 * Markup only. The behaviour lives in the bootstrap script in BaseLayout.astro,
 * because switching the theme has to happen BEFORE first paint and a script
 * down here in the body would flash the wrong palette on every navigation.
 * Keeping both halves in one inline script also keeps the page at one script
 * tag rather than two.
 *
 * HIDDEN UNTIL JAVASCRIPT CONFIRMS IT WORKS. The `hidden` attribute is in the
 * server-rendered HTML and the bootstrap removes it. A control that silently
 * does nothing is worse than no control, and anyone with JS disabled still gets
 * a correct dark theme from `prefers-color-scheme` -- they simply cannot
 * override it, so they are not offered a switch that cannot move.
 *
 * AN ICON, NOT THE WORD "DARK"/"LIGHT". The label used to be visible text, and
 * it cost 55px in a header that had 13px of room left on a 360px phone. Making
 * it a 24px glyph is what buys the social icons their place on mobile.
 *
 * THAT ALSO DELETED A WHOLE CLASS OF BUG. The visible label alternated between
 * two words of different widths, so the button resized on every click and
 * shoved the rest of a sticky header sideways. The old fix was an invisible
 * second label stacked in the same grid cell, sizing the button to its widest
 * state. A fixed-size icon cannot do that at all, so the sizer, the grid, and
 * the note explaining why `ch` units were wrong are all gone with it.
 *
 * The accessible name is NOT here. The bootstrap sets `aria-label` to "Switch
 * to dark theme" or "Switch to light theme", because only the resolved theme
 * knows which direction the button goes. `aria-label` on a button overrides
 * its contents, so the glyph is `aria-hidden` and there is deliberately no
 * visually-hidden text -- a second name would simply never be announced.
 *
 * THE GLYPH IS A SQUARE, NOT THE USUAL SUN AND MOON. DESIGN.md allows exactly
 * one radius in the system and it is the 2px focus ring; a circular contrast
 * icon would be the only round piece of our own chrome on the site. A square
 * split half-filled reads as the same "contrast" idea in the house material.
 * The third-party social marks keep their curves because altering a trademark
 * is not ours to do -- this is our own control, so it follows our own rules.
 */
---

<button
  type="button"
  id="theme-toggle"
  hidden
  class="flex h-6 w-6 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center border border-line text-ink hover:border-accent hover:text-accent-hover"
>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" class="h-[12px] w-[12px]">
    <path d="M2 2h20v20H2z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" />
    <path d="M12 2h10v20H12z" fill="currentColor" />
  </svg>
</button>
